{
  "rules": [
    "When sentiment is consistently near zero (0.00) while price history shows a strong downtrend or consolidation, the model systematically underestimates volatility and fails to capture sudden mean-reversion spikes; apply a variance correction factor of +15% to forecasted ranges.",
    "The model exhibits a severe lag in reacting to sentiment shifts from negative (-1.00) to positive (1.00), often predicting a slow, linear recovery instead of an immediate price surge; correct by accelerating the forecast slope immediately following a sentiment flip.",
    "In periods of high volatility with mixed sentiment, the model tends to smooth out extreme true outcomes, resulting in systematic overshoots on the upside and undershoots on the downside; apply a dampening coefficient of 0.85 to predicted peak values and 1.15 to predicted troughs.",
    "When the last 3 days of history show a trend reversal (e.g., rising then falling) but sentiment remains neutral, the model incorrectly projects continuation of the initial trend direction; reverse the forecast trajectory based on the most recent 3-day price momentum rather than long-term averages.",
    "The model consistently underestimates the magnitude of mean reversion events when sentiment history contains isolated spikes (0.50 or 1.00) amidst a flat baseline; increase the projected amplitude of correction phases by 20%."
  ]
}
